<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malnad</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Malenadu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Malnad is the hilly, forested and rain-soaked region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ghats">Western Ghats</a>, covering parts of districts such as Shivamogga, Chikkamagaluru, Hassan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kodagu">Kodagu</a>. It is known for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, areca nut and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keladi">Keladi</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayaka</a> dynasties both rose from chiefs of the Malnad hills.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malnad.jpg" alt="Malnad" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: KshitizBathwal · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lush_Green_Forest_of_Maldenad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Malenadu covers the western and eastern slopes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ghats">Western Ghats</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> and in part the Eastern Ghats. It is counted today as thirteen districts, though the districts first taken to make up the region were Shivamogga, Chikkamagaluru, Uttara Kannada, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kodagu">Kodagu</a>, Hassan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belagavi">Belagavi</a>. The origin of the name is unclear: in Kannada male without a retroflex l means mountain, giving mountainous land, while with a retroflex l it means rain, giving rainy land; the first form is the more common.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ghats">Western Ghats</a>, also called the Sahyadri ranges, block the south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> winds from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a>, so the region receives 1,000 to 3,800 millimetres of rain a year, and the village of Agumbe the highest rainfall in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>. Its forests act as sponges that feed the Sharavathi, Tunga, Bhadra, Kaveri, Kabini, Netravathi and other rivers long after the monsoon. Mullayyanagiri, at 1,925 metres the highest peak in Karnataka, rises in the Chandra Drona ranges, and the region's waterfalls include Jog Falls. Organised farming on land cleared from the forest has been practised there for a thousand years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Villages are scattered and remote, and an Area Development Board was created in 1991 to carry out projects in the region. Its protected areas, among them Kudremukh National Park, the Bhadra Wildlife Sanctuary and Nagarahole, shelter tigers, elephants, lion-tailed macaques, hornbills and king cobras, within a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ghats">Western Ghats</a> that ranks among the world's biodiversity hotspots. Its cooking, largely non-vegetarian and spicy, includes akki rotti, kadubu, neer dose and the pandi curry of Coorg.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Malenadu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Hoysalas were hill chiefs of the <b>Malnad</b>, the forested uplands of the Western Ghats in southern Karnataka.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hoysala-empire">Hoysala Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Keladi Nayakas began as local chiefs at Keladi in the <b>Malnad</b> hills, serving the Vijayanagara Empire as governors from around 1499.</blockquote><a href="/state/keladi-nayaka-kingdom">Keladi Nayaka kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
